    {
      "id": "1n1ykvGJwB7dLFt-RpQB3",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -980.1837742263758,
      "y": 138594.0319148313,
      "width": 1069.6756534169149,
      "height": 472.71020037363667,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "boH",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 544952784,
      "version": 90,
      "versionNonce": 140269360,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1767452129790,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "🔑 First, one golden rule (very important)\n\nSpring Security NEVER calls constructors magically.\nYOU call constructors.\nSpring only routes Authentication objects through filters & providers.\n\nSo the question is not “how does Spring know which constructor to call?”\nThe real question is:\n\nHow does Spring know WHEN this token represents “before authentication”\nvs “after authentication”?\n\nAnswer:\n👉 isAuthenticated flag + which constructor you used",
      "fontSize": 27.01201144992209,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "🔑 First, one golden rule (very important)\n\nSpring Security NEVER calls constructors magically.\nYOU call constructors.\nSpring only routes Authentication objects through filters & providers.\n\nSo the question is not “how does Spring know which constructor to call?”\nThe real question is:\n\nHow does Spring know WHEN this token represents “before authentication” vs “after authentication”?\n\nAnswer:\n👉 isAuthenticated flag + which constructor you used",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
